WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.506
In Lecture thirteen

2
00:00:04.506 --> 00:00:07.717
every projection needed a small system

3
00:00:07.717 --> 00:00:09.484
the normal equations.

4
00:00:09.564 --> 00:00:11.812
Today we look for axes where

5
00:00:11.892 --> 00:00:14.060
no system is needed at all.

6
00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:16.893
Here is a slanted grid.

7
00:00:16.893 --> 00:00:21.750
Its buttons are u, three, four, and v, four

8
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:22.290
two.

9
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.867
The target b is five, five.

10
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:29.931
On this grid it is one step of u

11
00:00:29.931 --> 00:00:32.680
then half a step of v.

12
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.357
A quick guess

13
00:00:34.357 --> 00:00:37.389
cast b's shadow on each button

14
00:00:37.389 --> 00:00:39.378
and add the shadows.

15
00:00:39.378 --> 00:00:42.884
The shadow on u is one point four u.

16
00:00:42.884 --> 00:00:46.390
The shadow on v is one point five v.

17
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.586
Added together, they overshoot far past b.

18
00:00:50.586 --> 00:00:53.700
The guess counts part of b twice.

19
00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.610
What would the buttons need

20
00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.536
for the shadows to add up to b?

21
00:00:59.536 --> 00:01:01.090
Pause and think.

22
00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.140
They need to be perpendicular

23
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.704
and of length one.

24
00:01:08.704 --> 00:01:11.750
Such vectors are called orthonormal.

25
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.245
Turn the square grid by

26
00:01:14.338 --> 00:01:18.255
the angle of a three, four, five triangle.

27
00:01:18.255 --> 00:01:21.612
Its first axis is q one, point six

28
00:01:21.612 --> 00:01:22.824
point eight.

29
00:01:22.824 --> 00:01:26.554
The second is q two, minus point eight

30
00:01:26.554 --> 00:01:27.580
point six.

31
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.372
Put them in the columns of Q.

32
00:01:30.372 --> 00:01:33.909
Each column dotted with itself gives one

33
00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:36.604
and the two together give zero.

34
00:01:36.604 --> 00:01:38.119
That is one line

35
00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:40.730
Q transpose Q is the identity.

36
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.231
Turning by Q keeps every

37
00:01:43.321 --> 00:01:45.383
length and every angle.

38
00:01:45.383 --> 00:01:48.700
A square of the grid stays a square

39
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.120
however large you draw it.

40
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.320
Now the shadows work.

41
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.563
Bring back b, five, five.

42
00:01:57.563 --> 00:02:02.428
b dot q one is three plus four, seven.

43
00:02:02.428 --> 00:02:07.168
b dot q two is minus four plus three

44
00:02:07.168 --> 00:02:08.540
minus one.

45
00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:11.751
Walk seven steps along q one

46
00:02:11.751 --> 00:02:14.907
then one step back along q two

47
00:02:14.907 --> 00:02:17.570
and you land exactly on b.

48
00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.332
All at once: x hat is Q transpose b.

49
00:02:21.332 --> 00:02:23.227
No system to solve

50
00:02:23.227 --> 00:02:26.923
because Q transpose Q is the identity

51
00:02:26.923 --> 00:02:30.240
and the normal equations collapse.

52
00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.112
That is why the guess failed

53
00:02:33.112 --> 00:02:35.634
u dot v is twenty, not zero.

54
00:02:35.634 --> 00:02:37.807
Slanted shadows overlap

55
00:02:37.807 --> 00:02:40.590
and part of b is counted twice.

56
00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.472
Here is a tempting shortcut

57
00:02:43.472 --> 00:02:46.020
perpendicular is what matters

58
00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.650
so the lengths can be anything.

59
00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:49.940
Test it.

60
00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.245
Double both columns of Q.

61
00:02:52.245 --> 00:02:55.172
The grid keeps its right angles

62
00:02:55.172 --> 00:02:58.985
but every square grows four times in area

63
00:02:58.985 --> 00:03:02.710
W transpose W is four times the identity.

64
00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.796
Now the dot product of b with the

65
00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:07.951
first column is fourteen.

66
00:03:07.951 --> 00:03:11.682
But the true coordinate is three and a half.

67
00:03:11.682 --> 00:03:13.340
Four times too big.

68
00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:17.307
So orthonormal means both: perpendicular

69
00:03:17.307 --> 00:03:18.718
and length one.

70
00:03:18.718 --> 00:03:20.659
Shrink the grid back

71
00:03:20.659 --> 00:03:23.570
and the shortcut is exact again.

72
00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.065
But the buttons we are given

73
00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:27.706
are rarely orthonormal.

74
00:03:27.706 --> 00:03:29.484
Back to the slanted grid.

75
00:03:29.552 --> 00:03:31.467
Gram-Schmidt straightens any

76
00:03:31.535 --> 00:03:32.971
basis with two moves

77
00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:34.886
subtract the shadows on the

78
00:03:34.954 --> 00:03:36.664
directions already built

79
00:03:36.664 --> 00:03:38.510
then divide by the length.

80
00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.619
Start with u, three, four.

81
00:03:41.619 --> 00:03:46.394
Its length is five, so q one is u over five

82
00:03:46.394 --> 00:03:48.940
point six, point eight.

83
00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.097
Now v, four, two.

84
00:03:51.097 --> 00:03:54.925
Its shadow on q one is v dot q one

85
00:03:54.925 --> 00:03:59.603
which is four, times q one: two point four

86
00:03:59.603 --> 00:04:01.410
three point two.

87
00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.636
Subtract the shadow.

88
00:04:03.636 --> 00:04:06.826
What is left is B, one point six

89
00:04:06.826 --> 00:04:08.796
minus one point two.

90
00:04:08.796 --> 00:04:12.643
It is perpendicular to the first button

91
00:04:12.737 --> 00:04:15.270
four point eight minus four

92
00:04:15.364 --> 00:04:17.240
point eight is zero.

93
00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:22.695
B has length two, so q two is B over two

94
00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:26.430
point eight, minus point six.

95
00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.071
Same plane, new axes.

96
00:04:29.071 --> 00:04:33.290
Straighten the grid along q one and q two.

97
00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:37.396
Now b's coordinates are dot products again.

98
00:04:37.396 --> 00:04:39.842
b dot q one is still seven.

99
00:04:39.842 --> 00:04:42.725
This q two points the other way

100
00:04:42.725 --> 00:04:45.520
so its dot product is plus one.

101
00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.336
Seven along q one, one along q two

102
00:04:49.336 --> 00:04:51.713
and again we land on b

103
00:04:51.713 --> 00:04:53.990
with no system solved.

104
00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.229
So: orthonormal columns make Q

105
00:04:57.318 --> 00:04:59.533
transpose Q the identity

106
00:04:59.622 --> 00:05:01.660
and every coordinate is

107
00:05:01.749 --> 00:05:03.610
a single dot product.

108
00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.794
Gram-Schmidt builds such

109
00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:07.627
columns from any basis

110
00:05:07.703 --> 00:05:09.460
subtract the shadows on

111
00:05:09.537 --> 00:05:11.217
what is already built

112
00:05:11.217 --> 00:05:13.280
then divide by the length.

113
00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:17.109
With more vectors it goes on the same way

114
00:05:17.192 --> 00:05:19.843
each new vector loses one shadow

115
00:05:19.926 --> 00:05:21.499
on every earlier q

116
00:05:21.499 --> 00:05:24.150
and what is left is normalized.

117
00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:28.064
Try one: run Gram-Schmidt on five

118
00:05:28.064 --> 00:05:30.221
zero and three, four.

119
00:05:30.221 --> 00:05:32.770
What are q one and q two?

120
00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.340
In part two we write down every

121
00:05:36.444 --> 00:05:39.040
number Gram-Schmidt used.

122
00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:42.467
They fill a triangular matrix R

123
00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:44.960
and A equals Q times R.
